.TH NOX 1 "September 2026" "NoxCrypt 1.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
nox \- hybrid classical/post-quantum file encryption and signatures
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nox
[\fB\-\-home\fR \fIDIR\fR]
[\fB\-\-faketime\fR \fIUNIX\fR]
\fICOMMAND\fR [\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.br
.B nox --version
.br
.B nox help
[\fICOMMAND\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B nox
is a small command-line tool for file encryption and detached
signatures. It is closer to
.BR age (1)
and
.BR minisign (1)
than to OpenSSL: auditable C, no network protocol, stdin/stdout, one
job per subcommand.
.PP
Every identity (key pair) carries a chosen
.IR suite :
one or two signing keys (Ed25519, ML-DSA-44) and one or two encryption
keys (X25519, ML-KEM-768). The default suite is hybrid, all four keys.
Encryption wraps a random file key for each recipient identity, or for
a passphrase; signatures are detached and cover a BLAKE2b hash of the
message.
.PP
Keys live in a keyring directory, selected by fingerprint prefix
(8 to 64 lowercase hex characters, case-insensitive). Secret keys are
always passphrase-protected with Argon2id and are never written out
in the clear. Binary output is refused on a terminal unless armored;
use
.B \-\-armor
or redirect to a file.
.PP
The wire format is defined in
.IR doc/SPEC.md
in the source tree. This build implements SPEC-v1.1 (draft, wire
version 0x01). It is unaudited; do not treat it as production
cryptography.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BR "gen " "[\fB\-c\fR \fICOMMENT\fR] [\fB\-\-sign\fR \fIALG\fR] [\fB\-\-enc\fR \fIALG\fR] [\fB\-\-pub\fR \fIFILE\fR] [\fB\-\-sec\fR \fIFILE\fR] [\fB\-a\fR] [\fB\-\-passphrase\-file\fR \fIFILE\fR]"
Generate an identity and store it in the keyring. Prints the
64-character fingerprint on stdout. The secret key always prompts for
a passphrase (asked twice).
.RS
.TP
.BR "\-\-sign " "\fIed25519\fR|\fImldsa44\fR|\fIhybrid\fR"
Signing keys to generate. Default
.BR hybrid ,
both algorithms.
.TP
.BR "\-\-enc " "\fIx25519\fR|\fImlkem768\fR|\fIhybrid\fR"
Encryption keys to generate. Default
.BR hybrid ,
both algorithms.
.TP
.BR "\-c " "\fICOMMENT\fR, " "\-\-comment " "\fICOMMENT\fR"
Free-form label, up to 1024 bytes of UTF-8 without NUL, CR, or LF.
.TP
.BR "\-\-pub " "\fIFILE\fR, " "\-\-sec " "\fIFILE\fR"
Also write the public or secret key to
.IR FILE .
Output to a terminal is armored automatically.
.RE
.TP
.BR "encrypt " "[\fB\-r\fR \fIFP\fR]... [\fB\-R\fR \fIFILE\fR]... [\fB\-p\fR] [\fB\-a\fR] [\fB\-o\fR \fIFILE\fR] [\fB\-\-passphrase\-file\fR \fIFILE\fR] [\fIIN\fR]"
Encrypt
.I IN
(stdin if omitted or
.BR \- )
for up to 16 recipients. Each identity recipient gets the recipient
block that matches its suite: hybrid, X25519-only, or ML-KEM-only.
.RS
.TP
.BR "\-r " "\fIFP\fR, " "\-\-recipient " "\fIFP\fR"
Recipient fingerprint prefix from the keyring. Repeatable.
.TP
.BR "\-R " "\fIFILE\fR, " "\-\-recipient\-file " "\fIFILE\fR"
Recipient public key file (binary or armored). Repeatable.
.TP
.BR "\-p" ", " "\-\-passphrase"
Passphrase recipient. It must be the sole recipient; the passphrase
is asked twice.
.RE
.TP
.BR "decrypt " "[\fB\-i\fR \fIFP\fR] [\fB\-p\fR] [\fB\-o\fR \fIFILE\fR] [\fB\-\-passphrase\-file\fR \fIFILE\fR] [\fIIN\fR]"
Decrypt
.IR IN .
With
.BR \-i ,
unlock the matching secret key (prompting for its passphrase) and
unwrap the first recipient block of the same suite. With
.BR \-p ,
unwrap a passphrase ciphertext. If
.B \-i
is omitted and the keyring holds exactly one secret key, it is used.
A recipient block that names the identity but uses the wrong suite is
refused rather than unwrapped.
.TP
.BR "sign " "[\fB\-i\fR \fIFP\fR] [\fB\-a\fR] [\fB\-o\fR \fIFILE\fR] [\fB\-\-passphrase\-file\fR \fIFILE\fR] [\fIIN\fR]"
Create a detached signature over
.IR IN .
One signature packet is emitted per signing key of the identity. The
.B \-i
defaulting works as in
.BR decrypt .
.TP
.BR "verify " "[\fB\-k\fR \fIPUBFILE\fR | \fB\-i\fR \fIFP\fR] \fISIG\fR [\fIMESSAGE\fR]"
Verify the detached signature
.I SIG
over
.I MESSAGE
(stdin if omitted). Every signing key of the signer's public identity
must verify. On success prints only
.BR "Good signature" .
Without
.B \-k
or
.BR \-i ,
the signer's key is looked up in the keyring by the fingerprint
stored in the signature.
.TP
.BR "list " "[\fIQUERY\fR]"
List the keyring, sorted by fingerprint, one three-line block per key:
.RS
.PP
.nf
<64-hex-fingerprint> <YYYY-MM-DD> <sec|pub>
sign: <alg>...; enc: <alg>...
"<comment>"
.fi
.PP
The date is UTC.
.B sec
means the secret key is present,
.B pub
means public only. The optional
.I QUERY
matches a fingerprint prefix or a comment substring
(case-insensitive).
.RE
.TP
.BR "export " "[\fB\-s\fR] [\fB\-a\fR] [\fB\-o\fR \fIFILE\fR] \fIFINGERPRINT\fR"
Write the public key for
.IR FINGERPRINT
(a prefix of at least 8 hex characters) to stdout or
.IR FILE .
With
.BR \-s ,
export the (still passphrase-protected) secret key instead.
.TP
.BR "import " "[\fB\-\-passphrase\-file\fR \fIFILE\fR] \fIFILE\fR"
Import a public or secret key file (binary or armored) into the
keyring. Imports are verified before they are stored: self-signatures
must check, and a secret key must unlock with the given passphrase
and match its public half. Prints the fingerprint on stdout.
.TP
.BR "delete " "\fIFINGERPRINT\fR"
Remove the public and secret key files for
.IR FINGERPRINT .
.TP
.B info
Show version and library information, one
.IR "key: value"
pair per line: version, author, spec revision, keyring path, and the
compiled-in signing, encryption, KDF, AEAD, and hash algorithms.
.TP
.BR "help " "[\fICOMMAND\fR]"
Show global help, or the help text for one command.
.SH KEY SUITES
.B gen
picks signing and encryption keys independently:
.PP
.nf
--sign ed25519|mldsa44|hybrid    (default: hybrid)
--enc  x25519|mlkem768|hybrid    (default: hybrid)
.fi
.PP
.B hybrid
means both algorithms of that kind. Common combinations are the
default hybrid suite, classic
.RB ( "--sign ed25519 --enc x25519" ),
post-quantum
.RB ( "--sign mldsa44 --enc mlkem768" ),
and mixed pairs such as Ed25519 signing with ML-KEM-768 encryption.
.PP
Encryption always matches the recipient's suite: a hybrid identity is
encrypted to with the combined X25519+ML-KEM-768 wrap, a single-suite
identity with its own X25519 or ML-KEM-768 wrap. Signatures likewise
carry one packet per signing key of the signer. There is no way to
talk a hybrid identity into single-algorithm operation; a different
suite is a different identity with a different fingerprint.
.PP
ML-KEM-768 is a key encapsulation mechanism, not an encryption
scheme: in every mode it only produces a shared secret, which is
hashed with a transcript into an XChaCha20-Poly1305 key that locks
the random file key. See
.IR doc/SPEC.md ,
section 6, for the exact derivations.
.SH EXAMPLES
Generate the default hybrid identity:
.PP
.nf
nox gen \-c 'alice@example.com'
.fi
.PP
Generate a classical-only identity and a post-quantum one:
.PP
.nf
nox gen \-\-sign ed25519 \-\-enc x25519 \-c classic
nox gen \-\-sign mldsa44 \-\-enc mlkem768 \-c pq
.fi
.PP
Encrypt and decrypt:
.PP
.nf
nox encrypt \-r 0123abcd \-a \-o secret.asc message.txt
nox decrypt \-i 0123abcd \-o message.txt secret.asc
.fi
.PP
Passphrase-only encryption (no identities):
.PP
.nf
nox encrypt \-p \-a \-o secret.asc message.txt
nox decrypt \-p \-o message.txt secret.asc
.fi
.PP
Sign and verify:
.PP
.nf
nox sign \-i 0123abcd \-a \-o message.sig message.txt
nox verify message.sig message.txt
.fi
.PP
Pipes and an alternate keyring:
.PP
.nf
printf 'piped' | nox encrypt \-r 0123abcd \-a | \\
    nox decrypt \-i 0123abcd > out.txt
nox \-\-home /tmp/demo list
.fi
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $NOX_HOME/.nox
Keyring directory if
.B NOX_HOME
is set, else
.IR ~/.nox ,
mode 0700.
.TP
.I <fingerprint>.pub
Public identity, mode 0600.
.TP
.I <fingerprint>.sec
Passphrase-wrapped secret identity, mode 0600.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NOX_HOME
Use
.I $NOX_HOME/.nox
as the keyring directory. Overridden by
.BR \-\-home .
.TP
.B HOME
Home directory used to locate the default keyring.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Runtime or cryptographic failure; the reason is printed on stderr as
.BR "nox: ..." .
.TP
.B 2
Usage error.
.SH SECURITY
Decryption and verification fail closed: malformed input, truncated
packets, unknown critical packets, trailing bytes, and suite
mismatches are errors, never warnings. A recipient block that names
your fingerprint with the wrong suite is refused as a downgrade
attempt.
.PP
Secret keys are protected by Argon2id (3 passes, 64 MiB, 1 lane) with
a random 16-byte salt per key, and plaintext secrets are wiped after
use. Streaming decryption writes each chunk as it authenticates, so a
failure in a later chunk cannot un-write earlier output; pipe through
a temporary file when all-or-nothing semantics matter.
.PP
Passphrases are read from /dev/tty with echo disabled, and terminal
state is restored even when interrupted. The
.B \-\-passphrase\-file
option exists for scripts and tests; prefer a pipe or a file with
mode 0600 over process arguments or the environment, which leak.
.PP
This software has not been audited. Prefer the default hybrid suite
unless you have a concrete reason (size, policy, hardware) to pick a
single algorithm, and keep those reasons, not habit, in charge.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR age (1),
.BR minisign (1)
.PP
.I doc/SPEC.md
and
.I doc/GUIDE.md
in the source distribution.
